(a) Subsections (a)(1) through (a)(5) of this section contain generally acceptable compliance alternatives illustrating principles which shall be applied to the rehabilitation of existing buildings by the building inspector in the city.
(1) Compliance alternatives for an inadequate number of exits include, but are not limited to, the following:
a. Providing connecting fire exit balconies acceptable to the building inspector between buildings;
b. Providing alternate exit or egress facilities leading to safety outside the building or to a place of safe refuge in the building or an adjoining building as acceptable to the building inspector;
c. Providing exterior fire escapes, as acceptable to the building inspector, where the providing of enclosed interior or enclosed exterior stairs is not practical; or
d. Installing early fire warning and fire suppression systems.
(2) Compliance alternatives for excessive travel distances to an approved exit include, but are not limited to, the following:
a. Installing an approved smoke detection system throughout the building;
b. Installing an approved complete automatic fire suppression system;
c. Subdividing the exit travel route with smoke-stop doors acceptable to the building inspector;
d. Increasing the fire resistance rating of corridor walls and doors; or
e. Providing additional approved means of escape.
(3) Compliance alternatives for unenclosed or improperly enclosed exit stairways or vertical shafts include, but are not limited to, the following:
a. Improving enclosure of exit stairway;
b. Adding a partial fire suppression system;
c. Adding a sprinkler draft curtain; or
d. Adding a smoke detection system.
(4) Compliance alternatives for inadequate or a total lack of fire partitions or fire separation walls shall be as set forth in subsection (a)(3) of this section.
(5) Compliance alternatives for a lack of required protection of openings in exterior walls where a fire exposure is a risk include, but are not limited to, the following:
a. Improving fire resistance of existing openings and protecting them with fire-rated windows or doors, as appropriate;
b. Sealing the openings with fire-rated construction, as approved by the building inspector; or
c. Installing an approved fire suppression system.
(b) Any consideration of the compliance alternatives provided in subsection (a) of this section should take into account all existing and proposed conditions to determine their acceptability. The compliance alternatives are not all-inclusive and do not preclude consideration and approval of other alternatives by the building inspector or his/her designee.
(Prior Code, § 30-13)

