814:
The public officer is hereby authorized to exercise the following powers in addition to the other powers granted and set forth in this chapter:
(1) To investigate the dwelling conditions in the city in order to determine which dwellings, buildings, or structures therein are unfit for human habitation, are unfit for current commercial, industrial, or business use, or are vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes;
(2) To administer oaths and affirmations, to examine witnesses, and to receive evidence;
(3) To enter upon premises for the purposes of making examinations; provided, however, that such entries shall be made in such a manner as to cause the least possible inconvenience to the persons in possession of such premises;
(4) To appoint and fix the duties of such officers, agents, and employees as he/she deems necessary to carry out the purposes of this chapter; and
(5) To delegate any of his/her functions and powers under this chapter to such officers and agents as he/she may designate.
(Prior Code, § 42-21)

826:
Before a building or structure can be reoccupied, the following are required:
(1) The building or structure must be fully returned to compliance with building/fire codes;
(2) The building or structure must be reinspected by the public officer and/or building official;
(3) Approval of required plans, if applicable, historic preservation, if applicable, request and purchase of all required building permits, subtrade permits, etc., as may be required, for compliance with construction codes; and
(4) No utilities shall be turned on until the building or structure is in full compliance with construction codes; provided that temporary service is permitted if necessary for construction work to bring such building or structure back in to compliance with construction codes.
(Prior Code, § 42-11)

834:
Each of the following places, conditions or things is declared to constitute a public nuisance, and whenever the housing official determines that any of these conditions exist upon any premises, such officer may require or provide for the abatement thereof pursuant to this article:
(1) Stagnant water on premises;
(2) Any dead or decaying matter, weeds, vegetation or any fruit, vegetable, animal or rodent upon premises which is odorous or capable of causing disease or annoyance to the inhabitants of the city;
(3) The generation of smoke and fumes in sufficient amount to cause an odor or annoyance to the inhabitants of the city;
(4) The pollution of public water or the injection of matter into the sewage system which is damaging thereto;
(5) Maintaining a dangerous or diseased animal or fowl;
(6) Obstruction of a public street, highway or sidewalk without a permit;
(7) Loud or unusual noises which are detrimental or annoying to the public, including, without limitation, unusual loud disturbances in or around churches or multiple-family complexes such as loud music and other activities in swimming pool and clubhouse areas;
(8) All walls, trees, building or other structures that may endanger persons or property;
(9) Any business or building where illegal activities are habitually and commonly conducted in such a manner as to reasonably suggest that the owner or operator of the business or building is aware of the illegal activities and has failed to reasonably attempt to prevent such activities;
(10) Unused iceboxes, refrigerators and the like, unless the doors, latches or locks thereof are removed;
(11) Any trees, shrubbery or other plants or parts thereof which obstruct clear, safe vision on roadways and intersections of the city;
(12) Any premises, dwelling unit or other structure, place or vacant lot permitted to deteriorate in appearance or condition so as to be degrading to the surrounding premises;
(13) The keeping of used or dilapidated furniture, appliances, machinery, bicycles, automobiles, automobile parts and other toys, junk, debris or other equipment, that are either in an inoperable condition or are wholly or partially wrecked, junked, or dismantled, upon premises which are not completely enclosed within a building;
(14) Tall grass, weeds and bushes, other undergrowth, and trash and debris of any type upon the premises of any dwelling unit or other structure, place or vacant lot;
(15) The slaughtering and butchering of animals, except when done in an enclosed area free from public view;
(16) Any outdoor lighting which is detrimental or annoying to the public or directly interferes with the quiet enjoyment of surrounding residential premises; and
(17) Any other condition constituting a nuisance under state law.
(Prior Code, § 48-2)

837:
(a) It shall be unlawful for the owner or occupant of any property within the city to have, place or allow the existence of any of the following on such premises to the extent and in a manner that could threaten or endanger the health, safety and welfare or could adversely affect and impair the economic value of adjacent property:
(1) Any accumulation of litter, debris, refuse, garbage, garden trash, rubbish or animal excrement on any front, side or rear yard or any porch, underneath any building or in any accessory building;
(2) Any objects, including, but not limited to, junk materials, machinery and equipment, appliances or other household items, building materials or piles of dirt in the front yard of any property or on a front porch of any structure located thereon;
(3) Any objects, including, but not limited to, junk materials, machinery and equipment, appliances or other household items, building materials or piles of dirt on the side or rear yards, or on side or rear porches or underneath any building or in any accessory building that is visible from a street, unless stored in an enclosed building;
(4) Any overgrown grass or weeds of a height six inches or more or any other unkempt vegetation in any yard, front, side or rear, of all improved property of two acres or less, or any overgrown grass or weeds of a height 12 inches or more or any other unkempt vegetation in any yard, front, side or rear, of all unimproved property of less than two acres or improved property larger than two acres, except that grass areas abutting and within 50 feet of any city street shall be maintained at a height not exceeding six inches;
(5) Any storage building on the front yard or any dilapidated storage building on the side or rear yard;
(6) Any automobile, truck or vehicle tires on the front, rear or side of any property for a period exceeding 45 days, unless stored in an enclosed building;
(7) Any clothesline or other outdoor apparatus used for the drying of clothes or other fabrics used for household or business use in the front yard of any residence or business.
(8) Property owners shall be prohibited from allowing kudzu (pueraria lobata) to grow on their property within ten feet of any adjacent property line or right-of-way.
(b) It shall be unlawful to place, keep, store or allow an automotive vehicle, boat or trailer-type vehicle on the front yard of any city property unless placed on an established paved or graveled driveway.
(c) It shall be unlawful to place, keep, store or allow to remain any derelict automotive vehicle or derelict boat or trailer-type vehicle or parts thereof on a front yard or the established paved or gravel driveway of a front yard, and it shall be unlawful to place, keep, store or allow to remain any derelict automotive vehicle or derelict boat or trailer-type vehicle or parts thereof on the side or rear yards of any city property if visible from a street or adjoining property unless contained in a properly enclosed and roofed accessory building which meets all appropriate zoning requirements of the city. For purposes of this section, the term "derelict automobile, boat or trailer-type vehicle" means a vehicle that is not operable due to lack of repairs or is unsafe to operate due to lack of repairs or damage and/or which constitutes a nuisance pursuant to this article.
(Prior Code, § 49-1; Ord. of 2-4-2003; Ord. No. 09-18-01, § 1, 10-2-2018)

859:
The Mayor and Commission of the City of Cornelia find that special events as herein defined, are a medium through which individuals may conduct certain lawful economic and non-economic activities on the public properties, streets, sidewalks, and rights-of-way lying within the City of Cornelia, Georgia, and that necessary to the protection of public health, safety, and welfare of the citizens of Cornelia. By enacting this chapter, the Mayor and Commission of the City of Cornelia intend to:
(1) Balance the rights of individuals, groups, organizations, clubs, associations, or other entities to conduct such special events;
(2) Protect the public health, safety, and welfare;
(3) Reduce traffic and pedestrian hazards;
(4) Reduce the possible adverse effect of such special events on the inhabitants and economic interests located in the City of Cornelia;
(5) Protect property values within the City of Cornelia by minimizing the possible adverse effects of such special events;
(6) Promote economic development; and
(7) Ensure the fair and consistent enforcement of special events regulations.
(Ord. No. 10-24-02, § 1, 11-5-2024)
